WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:05.230
It’s hard to imagine that the man responsible
for the Nazi’s deadly V2 weapon was also

00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:09.130
responsible for NASA’s majestic Saturn V
rocket.

00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:10.559
We have liftoff.

00:00:10.559 --> 00:00:12.459
Liftoff on Apollo 11.

00:00:12.459 --> 00:00:16.710
Wernher von Braun is one of the most CONTROVERSIAL
scientists in history.

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:18.779
He wasn’t just any scientist at NASA.

00:00:18.779 --> 00:00:21.689
He was THE scientist who got us to the moon.

00:00:21.689 --> 00:00:26.810
So, how did the man who worked for Adolf Hitler
become an American hero?

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:33.110
Werner von Braun was born on March 23, 1912
in the small town of in present-day Poland

00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:34.649
which was then part of the German Empire.

00:00:34.649 --> 00:00:35.649
(Prussia)

00:00:35.649 --> 00:00:36.649
He came from a wealthy, aristocratic family.

00:00:36.649 --> 00:00:39.460
When he was young, the Braun family settled
in Berlin where his father worked at the Ministry

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:43.629
of the Interior.

00:00:43.629 --> 00:00:48.100
His mother encouraged his curiosity by giving
him a telescope when he was a teenager.

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:51.350
It helped fuel his interest in astronomy and
space.

00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:54.780
You’d think that as a rocket scientist he
would have aced math and physics.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:58.719
But he didn’t do all that well in school
- at least not at first.

00:00:58.719 --> 00:01:03.489
The turning point came when he read The Rocket
into Planetary Space by rocket pioneer Hermann

00:01:03.489 --> 00:01:08.570
Oberthand decided to apply himself to calculus
and trigonometry so he could master the physics

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:10.020
of rocketry.

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:14.240
While studying mechanical engineering at the
Technical University of Berlin, von Braun

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:19.140
joined an amateur rocket association and caught
the attention of the German army which recognized

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:20.140
his talent.

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:24.680
They offered to finance his PhD research if
he helped develop liquid-fuel rockets for

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:25.909
the military.

00:01:25.909 --> 00:01:30.890
He got his doctorate in 1934, a year after
Hitler came to power.

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:36.090
With war on Hitler’s mind, rocketry became
a top priority for the German government.

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:41.729
And who better to lead the program than von
Braun, whose ambitions were out of this world?

00:01:41.729 --> 00:01:46.170
While a student, he attended a presentation
by Swiss physicist Auguste Piccard, famous

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:48.290
for his hydrogen balloon flights.

00:01:48.290 --> 00:01:52.790
Afterward, von Braun approached Piccard and
said: "You know, I plan on traveling to the

00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:53.790
Moon at some time."

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:57.659
Piccard is said to have responded with encouragement.

00:01:57.659 --> 00:02:01.789
Although he would never go to space himself,
von Braun is responsible for getting the first

00:02:01.789 --> 00:02:06.439
humans to the moon on the Apollo 11 mission
in 1969.

00:02:06.439 --> 00:02:11.300
At the time, the American public knew very
little if anything about his past as a member

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:14.130
of the Nazi Party and an SS officer.

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:19.340
There is no existing photo of von Braun in
which he can clearly be seen wearing his uniform.

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:24.251
The closest is this photo of him wearing the
Nazi party badge on his suit lapel, although

00:02:24.251 --> 00:02:26.450
even this isn’t all that clear.

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:30.780
The U.S. government largely hid his background
until after his death.

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:35.320
He was among hundreds of German scientists,
engineers, and technicians recruited as part

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:38.750
of “Operation Paperclip” at the end of
WWII.

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:43.360
Although President Truman forbade recruiting
Nazi members or supporters, U.S. intelligence

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:48.940
agencies eliminated or whitewashed incriminating
evidence of possible war crimes from their

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:49.940
records.

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:52.030
Von Braun was the star recruit.

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:57.130
He was the brains behind the Nazi’s V2 ballistic
missile – the first object to go into space

00:02:57.130 --> 00:03:03.690
- defined as 62 vertical miles out or 100
km - and effectively launched the space age.

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:08.800
Von Braun led the team that developed the
V2 at a secret laboratory at Peenemünde located

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:10.740
on a northern German island.

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:14.680
From there, they built and tested the rockets
by launching them into the Baltic Sea.

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:20.430
The V in V2 stands for the German word Vergeltungswaffen
which means “Vengeance”.

00:03:20.430 --> 00:03:22.780
This was Vengeance Weapon Number 2.

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:28.180
V2s attacked Allied cities in retaliation
for the Allied bombings against German cities.

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:32.460
They were used in the latter stages of the
war and were a last-ditch effort by the Germans

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:33.790
to ward off defeat.

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:36.490
The V2 was a totally new kind of weapon.

00:03:36.490 --> 00:03:40.500
It was the world's first large-scale liquid-propellant
rocket.

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:45.880
Instead of weapons with 100 pounds of thrust,
it had 55,000 pounds of thrust.

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:50.870
The Nazis launched over 3,000 V2s equipped
with a one-ton warhead, targeting Western

00:03:50.870 --> 00:03:53.370
Europe, mostly Britain and Belgium.

00:03:53.370 --> 00:04:00.000
They flew at more than 3,000 miles an hour
or 4,800 km/h and could reach London in five

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:01.000
minutes.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:05.140
Because they came in at over three times the
speed of sound, the V2 made absolutely no

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:07.020
sound until after it hit.

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:11.710
So, it crashed and exploded without warning
and is estimated to have killed between 5,000

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:14.430
to 9,000 civilians and soldiers.

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:18.980
Yet more than twice as many people are believed
to have died producing them.

00:04:18.980 --> 00:04:23.400
The Nazis forced prisoners from concentration
camps to build the rockets at the Mittelwerk

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:28.340
factory underneath a mountain in central Germany
to avoid enemy air attacks.

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:33.170
Prisoners did 12 hours a day of backbreaking
labor, pushing heavy equipment, in areas filled

00:04:33.170 --> 00:04:37.460
with dust and noxious gasses from the blasting
to widen the tunnels.

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:40.810
Conditions were far worse than depicted in
this Nazi propaganda photo.

00:04:40.810 --> 00:04:43.140
They slept in the tunnels they dug.

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:47.170
Their only toilets were oil barrels cut in
half with boards over them.

00:04:47.170 --> 00:04:50.820
Because there weren’t enough, many relieved
themselves in the tunnels.

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:53.470
The stench was intolerable.

00:04:53.470 --> 00:04:58.790
Starvation and poor hygiene exacerbated outbreaks
of typhoid and tuberculosis.

00:04:58.790 --> 00:05:03.710
The Nazis recruited prisoners who had technical
skills such as welding to build the rockets.

00:05:03.710 --> 00:05:08.750
Those accused of sabotaging their production
were hanged from cranes above the rocket assembly

00:05:08.750 --> 00:05:09.750
lines.

00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:14.220
When von Braun was later asked by a reporter
if he was aware of the slave labor that built

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:15.890
the V2s, he said:

00:05:15.890 --> 00:05:17.510
“Well, you're misinformed.

00:05:17.510 --> 00:05:22.200
The slave camp was about 400 miles from where
I worked…I learned later on that there were

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:26.920
atrocities taking place there, but I was not
involved in this whole operation…I have

00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:29.470
nothing to hide, I have nothing to hide.”

00:05:29.470 --> 00:05:34.170
However, historians are certain Wernher von
Braun saw the horrific conditions with his

00:05:34.170 --> 00:05:35.400
own eyes.

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:40.470
Michael J. Neufeld, author of the book Von
Braun: Dreamer of Space, Engineer of War found

00:05:40.470 --> 00:05:44.640
through his research that von Braun was in
the tunnels at least a dozen times.

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:48.830
Neufeld said that when von Braun testified
at a war crimes trial in West Germany in the

00:05:48.830 --> 00:05:52.900
late sixties, he mentioned he’d been through
the underground sleeping quarters.

00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:57.420
Von Braun later claimed in interviews that
he really had no choice but to join the Nazis.

00:05:57.420 --> 00:06:01.830
Interviewer: I believe you were forced to
join the Nazi party as I understand it?

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:05.520
Von Braun: No this isn’t quite right.

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:12.970
I got a letter one fine day which said, ‘We
understand you would like to join the party’.

00:06:12.970 --> 00:06:15.830
And here’s the application form.

00:06:15.830 --> 00:06:21.850
But the circumstances were such that the message
would have been very loud and clear had you

00:06:21.850 --> 00:06:23.480
not sent it in.

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:27.630
He continued to stick to the line that he
was simply a scientist who wanted to pursue

00:06:27.630 --> 00:06:30.230
the noble goal of going into space.

00:06:30.230 --> 00:06:33.230
He would later recall that he never liked
Hitler, referring to him as “Hitler was

00:06:33.230 --> 00:06:37.210
still only a pompous fool with a Charlie Chaplin
mustache.”

00:06:37.210 --> 00:06:41.430
He was also vocal about the fact that he was
arrested by Nazi Germany’s secret police,

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:47.431
the Gestapo, in March 1944 after he went to
a party and while drunk, allegedly said that

00:06:47.431 --> 00:06:50.220
he foresaw the war ending badly for Germany.

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:54.610
This was akin to treason and he was imprisoned
for about two weeks until Hitler’s men released

00:06:54.610 --> 00:06:57.150
him to continue producing V2s.

00:06:57.150 --> 00:07:00.930
The following year, in 1945, Germany surrendered.

00:07:00.930 --> 00:07:05.480
Von Braun had been planning for the postwar
era and went south to American-occupied territory

00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:09.910
in the Alps, where he made the shrewd decision
to surrender.

00:07:09.910 --> 00:07:14.490
The Americans were more than happy to snap
up the brilliant mind behind Germany’s advanced

00:07:14.490 --> 00:07:16.150
military arsenal.

00:07:16.150 --> 00:07:19.590
The US and the Soviet Union had never seen
anything like it.

00:07:19.590 --> 00:07:24.470
They captured many V2s for research to develop
their own space programs, propelling the former

00:07:24.470 --> 00:07:27.170
allies into an arms race in the fifties.

00:07:27.170 --> 00:07:32.140
For fifteen years after the war, von Braun
worked with the U.S. Army to develop ballistic

00:07:32.140 --> 00:07:38.430
missiles and launch vehicles, first in Texas
before stationing in Huntsville, Alabama.

00:07:38.430 --> 00:07:43.360
While settled in America, he sailed to Europe
in 1947 to bring back his bride.

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:46.800
He married his first cousin, Maria, in southeastern
Germany.

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:48.770
He was 35, she was 18.

00:07:48.770 --> 00:07:50.230
They had three children.

00:07:50.230 --> 00:07:54.889
Here’s a model, my design for a four-stage
orbital rocket ship.

00:07:54.889 --> 00:07:59.610
He became one of the most prominent advocates
of space exploration in the fifties and was

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:02.900
even featured in a Disney TV show on space
travel.

00:08:02.900 --> 00:08:08.150
In 1958, President Eisenhower created the
National Aeronautics and Space Administration

00:08:08.150 --> 00:08:12.460
- NASA - a federal agency dedicated to space
exploration.

00:08:12.460 --> 00:08:17.199
The US didn’t want to fall behind the Soviets,
who surprised everyone by launching Sputnik,

00:08:17.199 --> 00:08:24.400
the world’s first artificial satellite and
man-made object into Earth’s orbit in 1957.

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:28.150
The following year, America answered by putting
its first satellite into space

00:08:28.150 --> 00:08:30.550
Explorer is in orbit

00:08:30.550 --> 00:08:33.170
Under the direction of von Braun and his team.

00:08:33.170 --> 00:08:36.690
America’s ambitions would be even bigger.

00:08:36.690 --> 00:08:40.680
President John F. Kennedy gave America the
ultimate challenge.

00:08:40.680 --> 00:08:46.620
I believe that this nation should commit itself
to achieving the goal before this decade is

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:51.810
out of landing a man on the moon and returning
him safely to earth.

00:08:51.810 --> 00:08:56.400
Von Braun became the chief architect of the
Saturn V rocket that would propel Americans

00:08:56.400 --> 00:08:59.120
to the lunar surface on July 20, 1969.

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:02.050
That’s one small step for man.

00:09:02.050 --> 00:09:08.060
One giant leap for mankind.

00:09:08.060 --> 00:09:11.730
Apollo 11 also fulfilled von Braun’s lifelong
ambition.

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:14.720
Though, he would have loved to have gone to
space himself.

00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:20.900
In 1970, when he prepared to leave Huntsville
to take a job with NASA in Washington, he

00:09:20.900 --> 00:09:22.660
addressed a crowd and said:

00:09:22.660 --> 00:09:27.170
“My friends, there was dancing in the streets
of Huntsville when our first satellite orbited

00:09:27.170 --> 00:09:28.170
earth.

00:09:28.170 --> 00:09:31.450
There was dancing again when the first Americans
landed on the moon.

00:09:31.450 --> 00:09:36.800
I’d like to ask you, don’t hang up your
dancing slippers.”

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:41.350
He left NASA two years later to work at a
private aerospace manufacturing company.

00:09:41.350 --> 00:09:45.840
He continued to work ever after being diagnosed
with kidney cancer.

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:50.010
The cancer caught up with him and he was too
ill to attend the White House when President

00:09:50.010 --> 00:09:54.940
Gerald Ford awarded him the country’s highest
science honor, the National Medal of Science

00:09:54.940 --> 00:09:56.940
in 1975.

00:09:56.940 --> 00:10:04.730
Two years later, on June 16, 1977, he died
of cancer at a hospital in Alexandria, Virginia.

00:10:04.730 --> 00:10:07.450
He was 65 years old.

00:10:07.450 --> 00:10:11.450
Von Braun was known for two achievements that
were polar opposites.

00:10:11.450 --> 00:10:16.750
One was part of Hitler’s inhumanity – the
other a giant leap for humanity.

00:10:16.750 --> 00:10:22.550
It’s easy to label someone a hero or a villain,
to see things as black and white.

00:10:22.550 --> 00:10:24.620
But real life is complicated.

00:10:24.620 --> 00:10:25.920
And so was von Braun.

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:31.670
What was clear is that above all else, von
Braun loved space.

00:10:31.670 --> 00:10:36.330
While a student, he read an article imagining
a trip to the moon and recalled:

00:10:36.330 --> 00:10:39.070
“It filled me with a romantic urge.

00:10:39.070 --> 00:10:40.370
Interplanetary travel!

00:10:40.370 --> 00:10:43.920
Here was a task worth dedicating one's life
to.

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:47.870
Not just stare through a telescope at the
Moon and the planets but to soar through the

00:10:47.870 --> 00:10:51.800
heavens and actually explore the mysterious
universe.

00:10:51.800 --> 00:10:55.589
I knew how Columbus had felt.”

00:10:55.589 --> 00:11:01.350
Von Braun achieved his dream because of his
determination to master calculus and trigonometry

00:11:01.350 --> 00:11:04.270
so he could understand the physics of rocketry.

